    "case_id": "88",
    "case_summary": "A 50-year-old female patient, who is not sexually active, presented with swelling in the left inguinal area accompanied by a discharging sinus extending from the labia majora to the left inguinal crease. She had previously undergone incision and drainage for an abscess in the left labia majora six months prior. The patient reported no history of cough, fever, or abdominal pain and had not been in close contact with anyone diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis in the past year. Laboratory tests showed negative results for HIV and VDRL infections. Chest and abdominal X-rays were normal. Ultrasonography indicated a bulky uterus with an unvisualized endometrial line, and no masses or cysts were found in the bilateral adnexae. Blood tests revealed leucopenia and an ESR of 55 at 2 hours. The patient subsequently underwent excision of the sinus tract in the labia via a suprapubic approach, using methylene blue for staining. A mass was excised from the retropubic region.\n\nHistopathology report showed sinus tract lined by chronic inflammatory cells, epitheloid cells, and Langhan’s giant cells on microscopic examination.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
